        'Limited series went to “DTF St. Louis”, the love triangle with a murder that Steven Conrad wrote and directed for HBO, with Jason Bateman as a local news anchor, David Harbour as a sign-language interpreter and Linda Cardellini between them. It won series, directing, writing and both supporting prizes (Harbour and Cardellini), ahead of the second season of “Beef”, “The Beast in Me”, “All Her Fault” and “Love Story”, the miniseries about John F. Kennedy Jr. and Carolyn Bessette. Sally Field won lead actress for “Remarkably Bright Creatures” —which was also best television movie— over Claire Danes, Carey Mulligan, Sarah Snook and Sarah Pidgeon.',
        '## Colbert, after the cancellation',
        'And then there is “The Late Show with Stephen Colbert”, which won best variety series **after** CBS cancelled it and after it aired its last episode in May. The Academy also rewarded the directing of that final episode. An award for a show that no longer exists is, depending on how you look at it, a tribute or a rebuke, and probably both: the room applauded Colbert the way you applaud someone leaving, not someone winning. “The Traitors” repeated as competition series and Alan Cumming as host; the live special was Bad Bunny\'s Super Bowl halftime show, and the pre-recorded one the return of “The Muppet Show”.',
        '## The ceremony',
        'It was hosted by Mariska Hargitay, the first woman to do so since Jane Lynch in 2011 and the first non-comedian since Angela Lansbury in 1993. She opened with a musical number set to “I Love Rock \'n\' Roll” and with a line the room laughed at: that she was proud to be the first woman to host in fifteen years, and that “imagine, for fifteen years Amy and Tina said no”. The best came at the end, when she parodied Nicole Kidman\'s famous AMC cinemas advert and Kidman protested from the stalls —“that\'s my bit!”— only for Hargitay to tell her to zip it, “movie lady, it\'s TV\'s turn tonight”. The worst was the clock: the show finished early and the last presenters had to stall on stage, a rare thing at an awards show famous for the opposite.',
        'Michael J. Fox received the Bob Hope Humanitarian Award for three decades of work against Parkinson\'s. It was, by some distance, the moment the Peacock Theater stood up with the least protocol. The audience, on the other hand, set another record, and not a good one: 5.9 million viewers on NBC, the lowest in the history of the ceremony.',
        '## What the spread of prizes says',
        'Apple TV ended the season as the most awarded platform, with 28 Emmys counting the technical ceremonies. Six years ago it launched with a handful of shows; today it takes comedy, drama actress and drama writing, and the record of the night. HBO keeps the prestige —drama and limited series, the two categories that weigh most— and Netflix, which spends more than anyone, once again leaves with less than it hoped for: its big bets (“The Beast in Me”, “Beef”, “Nobody Wants This”) stayed at nominations, save for Rhys\'s Emmy.',
        'From here, an archivist\'s observation: five of this year\'s big prizes went to series that did not exist two years ago, and the most awarded comedy in Emmy history, counted by statuettes, is one season old. The television that gets rewarded renews itself faster than it used to, and the television people watch live —the ceremony itself— less and less. For anyone keeping a catalogue, the first is a pleasant problem to have. The second is a warning.',
