        'It competed at Venice earlier this month, with a seven-minute ovation and, as of today, 93% on Rotten Tomatoes with an average of 8.3 out of 10 across the first fourteen reviews. The notices speak of a magnetic performance, of a descent into madness wearing a news anchor\'s face, and of a film more interested in the psychology of the spectacle than in the scandal. And there is already Oscar talk, which is what gets said in September about any Venice film, but which in his case, for the first time, does not sound like an exaggeration.',
