        'The word “cinematheque” suggests a small room with odd seasons, and that is only the visible part. Behind it is an industrial facility: climate-controlled vaults, a lab, a scanner, kilometres of shelving and a team spending the year on an unromantic task, which is stopping a chemically unstable material from destroying itself.',
        '## The physical problem',
        'Until 1951, films were shot on **cellulose nitrate**: a base that ignites at low temperature, that water does not put out because it generates its own oxygen as it burns, and that decomposes on its own if it does not burn. That is why nitrate is kept in separate modules behind firewalls, like ammunition. What came next, acetate, looked like the solution until **vinegar syndrome** appeared: the base hydrolyses, the print smells of vinegar, shrinks and the emulsion separates. Cold and low humidity do not cure it, but they slow it enough to postpone it for centuries.',
        'Then there is digital, which many people assume is eternal and is the opposite. A digital file does not degrade gradually: it is lost all at once, when the medium fails or when nobody can read the format any more. Preserving digital means copying to new media every few years, forever. Cheaper to start, more expensive to keep.',
