        'There is a comfortable explanation —“Spain dubs because audiences like it”— and a true one, which comes earlier and is less pleasant. On 23 April 1941, a decree of the Ministry of Industry and Trade banned the screening of films not spoken in Spanish, and required dubbing to be done in studios on national soil with Spanish staff. It was not an original idea: it copied fascist Italy, which had been doing the same for a decade.',
