    en: {
      kicker: 'Article · Festivals',
      title: 'Cine Archive will cover the 2026 Seville European Film Festival as photo press',
      lead: 'From 6 to 14 November, the festival’s 23rd edition opens with an animated “Carmen”, gives its Honorary Awards to Agnieszka Holland and Cartoon Saloon, and brings fifteen films to the Official Section. Cine Archive will be there accredited as photo press: here is what is on, and what we will be covering.',
      stats: [
        { value: '6–14 Nov', label: 'dates of the 23rd edition' },
        { value: '15', label: 'films in the Official Section' },
        { value: '2', label: 'Honorary Awards: Agnieszka Holland and Cartoon Saloon' },
        { value: '66', label: 'Andalusian titles, features and shorts' },
        { value: '48', label: 'projects at ÍNSULA, the new script market' },
      ],
      body: [
        'The Seville European Film Festival holds its 23rd edition from 6 to 14 November, and this year Cine Archive will be inside: accredited as **photo press**, with a camera at the photocalls, the galas and the audience Q&As. It is the first time the archive goes out to cover a festival in person, and it could hardly be another one: it is our city’s festival and, in the words of its director Manuel Cristóbal, “the fourth festival in Spain by number of spectators and the first in Andalusia”.',
        '## A “Carmen” to open',
        'The opening film is **“Viva Carmen”**, by the French director Sébastien Laudenbach, co-written with Santiago Otheguy: an animated reinvention of Bizet’s opera set in the Seville of 1840, which premiered at Cannes. The choice is no accident. Carmen is probably the most famous Sevillian in the world without ever having existed, and the screening comes with a new partnership with the Seville Opera Festival.',
        'Animation weighs more than ever this year. One of the two Honorary Awards goes to **Cartoon Saloon**, the Irish studio founded in Kilkenny in 1999 by Paul Young, Tomm Moore and Nora Twomey, the makers of “The Secret of Kells” (2009), “Song of the Sea” (2014) and “Wolfwalkers” (2020). It is the first time the festival gives this award to an animation studio, as part of a programme prepared with the Irish Embassy during Ireland’s presidency of the Council of the EU. The other goes to the Polish director **Agnieszka Holland**, with more than forty years as a director and screenwriter.',
        '## The Official Section',
        'Fifteen films compete for the Golden Giraldillo, many of them from the year’s big festivals: **“You Don’t Belong Here”**, by Florin Şerban, Golden Leopard at Locarno; **“Elephants in the Fog”**, by the Nepalese director Abinash Bikram Shah, Jury Prize in Un Certain Regard; **“The Unknown”**, by Arthur Harari, which was in competition at Cannes; **“Blaise”**, by Dimitri Planchon and Jean-Paul Guigue, best film in the Contrechamp section at Annecy; and **“The Idiot(s)”**, by Małgorzata Szumowska and Michał Englert, which screened at Toronto. Two of them are also their countries’ international Oscar hopefuls: “Elephants in the Fog” and the Swedish “Adult Supervision”, by Alex Schulman.',
        'The two Spanish films in competition are **“Taranta”**, by Samuel Nacar, a documentary having its world premiere in Seville that follows the reindustrialisation of the old Santana factory in Linares, and **“Tralovento”**, by Eloy Domínguez Serén. Out of competition there is “Anguita. El hombre de los tres siglos”, by Hugo Cabezas.',
        'The European Film Academy section brings names that need no introduction: **“Coward”**, by Lukas Dhont; **“Fjord”**, by Cristian Mungiu; **“Fatherland”**, by Paweł Pawlikowski; and **“Yellow Letters”**, by İlker Çatak.',
        '## Andalusia, on screen and off',
        'The Panorama Andaluz brings together fifteen features, twelve of them documentaries, among them “Bajañí”, by Fernando Trueba, about the guitarist Niño Josele, and “200 vidas. Paco Rabal”, by Vanesa Benítez. The Andalusian programme adds up to 66 titles, features and shorts, and includes a tribute to the filmmaker Nonio Parejo (1950-2026) and the restored version of “Invasión travesti”, shot in Seville, for its 25th anniversary.',
        'On the industry side, the festival launches **ÍNSULA**, a script market that will bring 48 projects and 20 production companies to the Real Fábrica de Artillería on 9 November, and keeps FRAME, its industry area, led this year by the producer Rodrigo Espinel.',
        '## Where, and what we will cover',
        'The main venue is the Espacio Turina, and screenings are spread across MK2 Nervión Plaza, Cine Cervantes, Teatro Alameda, Odeón Plaza de Armas, Avenida 5 Cines and Cartuja Center CITE. The edition’s artwork is by the Sevillian illustrator José Luis Ágreda, who has devoted his posters to Antonio Banderas in “Tie Me Up! Tie Me Down!” and to “Metropolis”.',
        'Our work there will be mostly visual: the photocalls, the galas, the film teams and the atmosphere in the theatres. Photographs will be published in the archive during the festival, with short reports, and at the end we will go over the awards. If you are in Seville those days, see you in the queue. Checked on 27 October 2026; the full programme, with times and tickets, is on the festival’s website.',
      ],
    },
